reference = "Called PIR, polyiso, ISO, or isocyanurate (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyisocyanurate). Formula from http://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?Name=Polyisocyanurate&Units=SI&Units=SI&cTG=1&cTC=1&cTP= 1&cTR=1&cPI=1. Density range = 0.0264 to 0.096 g/cm3 at http://www.fpcfoam.com/polyiso-tech.html. Density = 0.0264, 0.0288, 0.048, 0.064, and 0.096 g/cm3 at http://www.fpcfoam.com/polyiso-tech.html. Density range = 0.033 to 0.32 g/cm3 at www.kingspantarec.com/en/pdf/tarecpir_datasheet.pdf. Density = 0.0482 g/cm3 for nominal 3.0 lb/ft3 density on ISC-C1 datasheet available from http://www.dyplastproducts.com/ISOC1_polyisocyanurate_insulation.htm. Nominal densities are available at 2.0, 2.5, 3, 4, 6, and 10 lb/ft3."

